-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Competing edits to the same visit produce complete, competing revisions. Each submission identifies its base revision by the revision ID of the state it was derived from. When two or more independent edits target the same base revision, they are stored as a competing set pending coordinator resolution. Resolution selects one submitted revision as current and retains all others for audit; no automatic merge occurs.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+This approach trades larger review queues for simplicity: unrelated field edits are treated as a coordinated change, and rules that would falsely "combine" fields across scientific relationships are not needed. The submitted visit remains a complete, authored unit with clear provenance.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Submission Structure
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+A submission carries:
+- **idempotency key**: a worker-supplied unique identifier allowing safe retry on connection loss
+- **visit ID**: the client-generated UUID identifying which visit is being edited
+- **base revision ID**: the revision ID of the state this edit was derived from (absent for initial submission, where base is the empty state)
+- **submitted values**: the complete proposed visit content (observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes)
+- **author ID**: the authenticated worker identity
+- **server receipt time**: assigned on acceptance
+
+## Current State and Review Status
+
+A visit can be in one of three states:
+
+**Single revision (resolved or no review needed)**: No competing edits exist. The system returns a current revision with its data and revision ID.
+
+**Competing revisions (unresolved)**: Multiple independent submissions target the same base revision ID. The system does not return a single "current" state; instead, it returns the full set of competing submission payloads with submission IDs and authors. The response indicates resolution status `unresolved`.
+
+**Coordinator-selected (resolved)**: A coordinator has selected one submission as authoritative. The system returns that submission as current revision and marks resolution status as `resolved`.
+
+Competing (unresolved) visits are excluded from export until a coordinator marks them resolved by selecting one revision.
+
+## Resolution Operation
+
+When a coordinator resolves competing revisions, they select one submitted revision ID as the authoritative current state. The resolution payload carries:
+- **selected submission ID**: which of the competing submissions becomes current
+- **coordinator ID**: the authenticated coordinator identity
+- **resolution time**: server-assigned
+
+The system persists the resolution alongside the selected submission and marks the visit as resolved. All competing submissions remain intact for audit.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync will track base revision IDs locally and display pending unresolved visits to field workers so they know which plots need attention. Review-export will present pairs or groups of competing revisions side-by-side for coordinator selection and exclude unresolved visits from the CSV export.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent edit handling | Whole-visit revisions (see [decisions/concurrent-edit-policy.md](decisions/concurrent-edit-policy.md)) | Field-aware three-way merge | Preserves submitted visit as a unit; avoids maintenance burden and risk of silent wrong combinations. Trade-off accepts larger review queue in exchange for simpler merge logic and preserved author intent. Details at the linked decision doc. |
